(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatological-infestations-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatological Infestations Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does a black widow spider bite present within the first thirty minutes?","choices":["A painless bite with induration and paraesthesia at the site, then a large area of erythema around it","A central violaceous area ringed by blanched skin and then a large asymmetric red area","Immediate burning and pain with local erythema, swelling and urticaria at the site","Localised erythema, piloerection and sweating around the bite, then crampy abdominal pain and muscle spasm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — headache, paraesthesia, nausea, vomiting, hypertension and seizures may follow. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What is the time course of a severe brown recluse bite?","choices":["Necrosis at six weeks, eschar at eight weeks, with no systemic symptoms at any stage","Vesicle formation in the first thirty-six hours, then healing over several weeks","Necrosis at two to three days, eschar between days five and seven, then deep ulcers; systemic symptoms at one to two days","Necrosis within thirty minutes, eschar within six hours, then deep ulcers by the end of the first day; systemic symptoms at once"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — nausea, vomiting, headache, fever and chills make up the systemic picture. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is the classic manifestation of stage 3, late persistent Lyme disease?","choices":["Cranial nerve palsies, meningitis and radiculopathies developing over days to weeks","A single expanding erythematous lesion with central clearing at the site of the bite","A blanching rash spreading centripetally from the ankles and wrists to the trunk","Monoarticular or oligoarticular arthritis affecting the knee or weight-bearing joints"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — subacute encephalopathy and acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans also occur. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which complications of scabies are named, and how are the persistent papules treated?","choices":["Necrotic ulceration requiring surgical reconstruction once the wound has become stable","Staphylococcal superinfection, post-scabietic papules treated with potent or intralesional steroid, and psychological effects","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, treated with supportive care while the kidney function recovers on its own","Acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans, treated with intravenous ceftriaxone over several weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — intralesional triamcinolone acetonide is given at 2.5 to 5 mg per millilitre. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does light microscopy with mineral oil show in cutaneous larva migrans?","choices":["Live and dead larvae, particularly in patients with folliculitis","Mites, ova and faecal pellets scraped from an unexcoriated burrow","Ovoid eggs visible within the lesion when viewed on dermoscopy","Nits attached at the base of hairs plucked from the affected area"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — histology shows larvae with an eosinophilic infiltrate. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Where is the hobo spider found and what is it often mistaken for?","choices":["The Great Lakes region, where it is mistaken for a harmless house spider entirely","The Pacific Northwest, where it is the predominant cause of necrotic arachnidism and is mistaken for the brown recluse","The American Midwest and Southeast, where it is the predominant necrotic spider and is mistaken for the tarantula","All but the most northern states, where it is mistaken for the black widow spider"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is brown with a grey herringbone pattern and bites from July to September. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What is the position on prophylactic antibiotics after a tick bite in a Rocky Mountain spotted fever area?","choices":["A single dose of doxycycline is recommended for every tick bite","A five-day course of doxycycline is recommended in endemic areas","Prophylaxis is recommended only for children under ten years of age","Prophylactic antibiotic therapy is not recommended"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — prevention rests on avoiding exposure, protective clothing, tick checks and DEET. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which organism causes Rocky Mountain spotted fever, and what are its vectors and risk factors?","choices":["Rickettsia rickettsii, carried by dog ticks, wood ticks and rodents; risk is higher in males, adults 40 to 64, children under 10 and rural dwellers","Borrelia burgdorferi, carried by ticks with birds and deer as dispersal animals; risk is higher in the northeastern and upper midwestern states","Xenopsylla cheopis, carried by rats; risk is higher in crowded conditions with poor sanitation","Cimex lectularius, carried in luggage and second-hand mattresses; risk is higher among travellers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is prevalent in southeastern and south central states in spring and early summer. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"How does the Rocky Mountain spotted fever rash spread?","choices":["It starts in the interdigital webs and wrists and spreads to the axillae and genitalia","It starts on the ankles and wrists and spreads centripetally over six to eighteen hours, involving palms and soles but sparing the face","It starts on the trunk and spreads outward centrifugally over several days, sparing the palms and the soles entirely","It starts at the site of the bite as a single expanding lesion with a clear centre"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — blanching macules or papules that may become petechial and purpuric. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which non-pharmacologic measures are required in scabies?","choices":["Engage a professional exterminator to eradicate the organism from the living space","Wash bedding and clothing at 60 degrees Celsius or bag them for fourteen days, and treat all contacts","Wash bedding at any temperature and treat only the patients who are currently symptomatic","Fumigate the home and vacuum all floors, carpets, upholstery and furniture thoroughly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — high heat is needed to kill both mites and ova. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is tungiasis?","choices":["Infestation caused by an adult female flea penetrating human skin to lay her eggs","Infestation caused by an animal hookworm larva migrating through human epidermis","Infestation caused by a mite tunnelling through the stratum corneum of the skin","Infestation caused by a flatworm larva penetrating skin during freshwater exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is caused by the family Tungidae. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How is body louse infestation diagnosed?","choices":["Skin scraping of an unexcoriated papule with mineral oil, examined under a microscope","Close examination of clothing seams for nits, and shaking clothing over white paper to see lice move","Wet combing of the scalp with water and conditioner using a fine-toothed nit comb","Microscopic examination of a plucked pubic hair to confirm nits at the hair base"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Head lice are found by combing and pubic lice by locating nits at the base of hairs. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Describe erythema migrans and when it appears.","choices":["A lesion over 5 cm, expanding, round or oval, with central clearing and a darker punctate centre, one week after the bite","A lesion under 1 cm, thread-like, linear or J-shaped, appearing four to six weeks after the infestation began","A blanching macular rash starting at ankles and wrists two to four days after fever onset","A serpentine raised trail advancing two to three centimetres a day with intense itch"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — also called the bull's eye rash, with fever, myalgia, arthralgia and lymphadenopathy. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"How does a severe local reaction to a sting differ from the typical reaction?","choices":["It produces extensive oedema at the site with induration lasting up to one week","It produces generalised urticaria, angio-oedema and bronchospasm within minutes","It produces central necrosis with an eschar forming between days five and seven","It produces cramping abdominal pain and muscle spasm within thirty minutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the typical reaction is immediate burning, erythema, swelling and urticaria. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which serological tests are used for Lyme disease and what does each add?","choices":["Skin scraping with mineral oil examined microscopically for the organism, ova and faeces","Dermoscopy looking for ovoid eggs within the lesion in an area of endemic exposure","Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay for immunoglobulin M and G, with the C6 peptide test more specific, and Western blot more specific still","Indirect immunofluorescence assay as the gold standard, though it is rarely diagnostic before the seventh day of illness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the immunoglobulin G assay carries the greater specificity. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"How is cercarial dermatitis managed?","choices":["With albendazole 400 mg by mouth daily for a course of three days, or ivermectin by mouth for one to two days","With overnight topical permethrin to the whole skin surface and a repeat dose at one week","With surgical excision or cryotherapy of each lesion together with tetanus prophylaxis","Symptomatically, with antihistamines, oatmeal baths, antipruritic lotions, aspirin for pain and topical or oral glucocorticoids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — proper washing and hygiene are also advised. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"How does tungiasis present?","choices":["A slowly advancing serpentine trail on the foot with intense pruritus for weeks","Papules enlarging over weeks to 4 to 10 mm, sometimes a yellow translucent nodule, on the feet and web spaces","Linear or clustered urticarial papules usually on the lower legs, with bullae in sensitised people","A row of grouped painless bites with a haemorrhagic punctum appearing overnight"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — pain, pruritus and even autoamputation of toes can follow. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What constitutes a positive burrow ink test?","choices":["Complete failure of the ink to adhere to the surface of the lesion at all","Coral-red fluorescence of the inked area seen under a Wood's lamp","A zigzag line running across and away from the lesion after ink is applied","Uniform uptake of ink across the whole surface of the suspected lesion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — blue or black ink is applied to the suspected lesion. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How is the brown recluse spider identified and where is it abundant?","choices":["A dense covering of urticating hairs; kept widely as a pet across the country","A dark fiddle or violin marking on the cephalothorax; abundant in the American Midwest and Southeast","A red hourglass on the underside of the abdomen; found in all but the most northern states","A grey herringbone pattern on the abdomen; predominant in the Pacific Northwest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is non-aggressive and shelters in closets, attics and stored bedding. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is the recommended position on school exclusion for head lice?","choices":["Children must remain out of school for two weeks after the treatment has been completed","Children must remain out of school until a repeat wet comb is entirely negative","A no-nit policy is not recommended by the American Academy of Pediatrics because of school absence","Children must remain out of school until every nit has been removed from the hair"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — nits may remain in the hair for months after successful treatment. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What does the presence of crusting or scaling on a lesion indicate?","choices":["That the epidermis has been affected","That the subcutaneous tissue is involved","That the lesion is infectious in origin","That the lesion has begun to resolve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that inference is what makes crust and scale useful morphological information. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the clinical triad of Rocky Mountain spotted fever, and how often is it present?","choices":["Fever, abdominal cramping and muscle spasm, present in nearly all patients","Fever, pruritus and a serpentine rash, present in about 30% of patients","Fever above 39.5 degrees Celsius, headache and rash, present in only about 60% of patients","Fever, arthritis and an expanding annular rash, present in about 90% of patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — fever comes first, with the rash two to four days after fever onset. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which caterpillar is described as the most poisonous, and what does its sting produce?","choices":["The processionary caterpillar, producing urticaria, angio-oedema and anaphylaxis","The tarantula, producing pruritus and granulomatous reactions from embedded hairs","The asp or puss caterpillar, producing an intensely painful sting and train-track purpura","The gypsy moth caterpillar, producing pruritic papules arranged in linear streaks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The gypsy moth caterpillar causes erucism and processionary caterpillars cause anaphylaxis. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the first-line oral antibiotic for Lyme disease, and what is used in children and pregnancy?","choices":["Azithromycin first line, with doxycycline reserved for those who cannot tolerate it","Intravenous ceftriaxone first line for all stages, with oral agents used only for prophylaxis","Doxycycline first line, with amoxicillin as the alternative first line for early erythema migrans","Amoxicillin first line, with doxycycline reserved for children and for pregnant patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — macrolides such as azithromycin are second line, over a ten to fourteen day course. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"How is the southern black widow spider identified, and what is in its venom?","choices":["A red hourglass on the underside of the abdomen; the venom contains the neurotoxin alpha-latrotoxin","A dark violin-shaped marking on the cephalothorax; the venom is cytotoxic and locally necrotising","A grey herringbone pattern on the abdomen; the venom causes necrotic arachnidism regionally","Barbed hairs shed from the abdomen; these embed in skin and eyes causing local reactions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — Latrodectus mactans is found in all but the most northern part of the country. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What characterises crusted, also called hyperkeratotic, scabies?","choices":["Widespread urticarial wheals appearing within minutes of contact and resolving within hours","Thick flaking scale containing millions of mites, with thickened nails and often no pruritus","A few discrete burrows in the finger webs with intense nocturnal itch and no visible scale","Indurated crusted nodules on the trunk and intertriginous areas of an infant or young child"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These patients are highly infectious despite the absence of itch. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How soon does pruritus appear after a first scabies infestation, and after a reinfestation?","choices":["Two to three days after the first infestation, but four to six weeks after reinfestation","Within twenty-four hours of either the first infestation or any later reinfestation","Three to twelve days after the first infestation and the same after reinfestation","Four to six weeks after the first infestation, but within two to three days after reinfestation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Many patients do not develop symptoms for three months. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What proportion of Hymenoptera stings produce a generalised systemic reaction?","choices":["Between 10% and 15%","Between 25% and 30%","Fewer than 1 in 10,000","Between 0.4% and 3%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — anaphylactic reactions include urticaria, angio-oedema and bronchospasm. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How is a bedbug infestation diagnosed and managed?","choices":["Diagnosis is by physical examination; management is symptomatic with a professional exterminator required","Diagnosis is by skin scraping under mineral oil; management is topical permethrin applied overnight","Diagnosis is by dermoscopy showing ovoid eggs; management is surgical excision or cryotherapy","Diagnosis is by serology; management is oral doxycycline for ten to fourteen days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — topical antiseptic or antibiotic for secondary infection, steroids or antihistamines for itch. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How do tarantulas cause human disease, and how is it managed?","choices":["Injected neurotoxin causes muscle spasm and abdominal pain; treat with calcium gluconate, narcotics and benzodiazepines","Injected cytotoxin causes central necrosis and eschar; treat with delayed surgical reconstruction","A bite causes painless induration and vesicles; treat supportively over several weeks","Shed hairs embed in skin or eyes causing pruritus, granulomas or conjunctivitis; treat with topical steroid and refer eyes to ophthalmology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — symptoms are generally mild and local. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"}]};
